        "original_id": "238eb4f2-037c-56d7-a9e8-84a853a923ab",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Products: Crepis, Isatis\n- Concepts: plant taxonomy, flowering plants, common names\n- Organizations: Asteraceae, Brassicaceae\n- Places: Mediterranean region, central Asia\n\nFacts:\n- Crepis is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants in the family Asteraceae.\n- Crepis is commonly known in some regions as hawksbeard or hawk's-beard.\n- Crepis superficially resembles the dandelion and usually has branching scapes with multiple heads, though solitary heads can occur.\n- The common name of Crepis should not be confused with the related genus Hieracium, which has a similar common name.\n- Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae.\n- Isatis is native from the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "5a128fae-3376-5d54-a201-664c978cdfc5",
        "text": "Page: Crepis. Sentence: Crepis, commonly known in some parts of the world as hawksbeard or hawk's-beard (but not to be confused with the related genus \"Hieracium\" with a similar common name), is a genus of annual and perennial flowering plants of the family Asteraceae superficially resembling the dandelion, the most conspicuous difference being that \"Crepis\" usually has branching scapes with multiple heads (though solitary heads can occur).\nPage: Isatis. Sentence: Isatis is a genus of about 30 species of flowering plants in the family Brassicaceae, native to the Mediterranean region east to central Asia.",
